V1_2_0_CUTOVER_SHA is empty, validates exit 0 with no post-v1.2.0 warning.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"If ambiguity occurs, operators receive no feedback that the post-v1.2.0 check is inactive; they could emit wrapper files with TF-023E-002-affected SHAs without the recommended v1.0.0 pin suggestion. Low probability event (~1/16M per commit) but silent failure class.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Emit a `warn` when `V1_2_0_CUTOVER_SHA` is empty AND `SCP_SHA` != `V1_0_0_SCP_SHA`, e.g.: 'could not resolve v1.2.0 cutover SHA (prefix 5d4341b); skipping post-v1.2.0 check'. This distinguishes the ambiguity/absent case from the legitimate fresh-repo case and gives operators a signal to investigate.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CORR-nit-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"nit\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"DISPATCH-NOTE §Scope says 'Output dirs created under $TMPDIR (cleaned by EXIT trap, not REPO_ROOT)' but test_scaffold_downstream.sh sets TMPDIR to a path inside REPO_ROOT (line 14: `mktemp -d \\\\\\\"${REPO_ROOT}/tmp.scaffold.XXXXXX\\\\\\\"`).\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"test_scaffold_downstream.sh line 14: `TMPDIR=\\\\\\\"$(mktemp -d \\\\\\\"${REPO_ROOT}/tmp.scaffold.XXXXXX\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\"`. The comment on lines 10-11 explains this is intentional to avoid macOS system-path guard rejection, but contradicts the DISPATCH-NOTE's 'not REPO_ROOT' language.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Documentation-code discrepancy could mislead a future maintainer into moving TMPDIR to the system /private/var/... path on macOS, breaking the system-path denylist test cases. No runtime impact today.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Update DISPATCH-NOTE §Scope (item 5, scaffolder unit tests) to say 'Output dirs created under $TMPDIR (set to a path within REPO_ROOT to avoid macOS system-path guard rejection; cleaned by EXIT trap)' to match the actual test implementation.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CORR-nit-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"nit\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"parse_entry_repo in check-invocation-log-entry.sh uses greedy .+ in the header regex `^###\\\\\\\\s+.+\\\\\\\\s+—\\\\\\\\s+([A-Za-z0-9._-]+...)@...`. The comment says 'keep this in sync with the branch-protection log emitter in 024B-extras'. If the 024B-extras emitter ever produces an entry where the prose between ### and — contains an em dash (U+2014), the greedy backtrack would extract from the wrong — separator, silently producing a wrong repo value.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"check-invocation-log-entry.sh lines 230-242: parse_entry_repo Python block. The regex `.+` is greedy; for input `### note — something — owner/repo@branch`, backtrack lands on the last ` — `, extracting `owner/repo` correctly. But for `### note — owner@host — owner/repo@branch`, backtrack lands on the last ` — `, extracting wrong repo. No cross-slice contract enforces the emitter's format at 024B-core review time.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Use a non-greedy `.+?` or a timestamp-anchored pattern (e.g., `[0-9TZ:.-]+`) before the em dash. Alternatively, document the exact emitter format requirement in a shared constant between 024B-core and 024B-extras so the sync dependency is explicit and machine-checkable.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CORR-nit-003\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"nit\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"scaffold-downstream.sh validates --scp-sha with `^[a-f0-9]{40}$` (lowercase-only hex), but the DISPATCH-NOTE spec says '40-char hex' without specifying case. The error message already says 'must be exactly 40 lowercase hex characters', so the requirement IS documented at runtime, but the plan-doc spec and DISPATCH-NOTE do not state 'lowercase'.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"scaffold-downstream.sh line 118: `grep -qE '^[a-f0-9]{40}$'`. DISPATCH-NOTE §Scope: '--scp-sha 40-char hex + HEAD lineage check'. Git always outputs lowercase SHAs; GitHub API returns lowercase. Practically harmless, but 